-- Passage rg9-context-clues-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('30314256-f0dd-5ce9-843b-ee6ddf9900fe', 'rg9-context-clues-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Bicycle in the Garage$q$, $q$The bicycle had been leaning against the back wall of Grandpa Teodor's garage for longer than Marisol had been alive. It was dilapidated: the seat was split, the tires were flat and cracked, and one pedal hung from a single loose bolt. When she asked if she could fix it, her grandfather laughed and handed her a box of tools.

The chain was the first problem. Years of damp air had left it corroded, its links crusted with orange rust so thick that they would not bend. Marisol soaked it in oil overnight and scrubbed each link with an old toothbrush until the metal showed gray again.

Cleaning the spokes was even more tedious. There were thirty-six of them on each wheel, and every one had to be wiped, checked and tightened by hand. After the first hour, Marisol was bored enough to count the spokes twice just to have something new to think about.

Then she spun the front wheel and watched it wobble from side to side. "It needs to be trued," Grandpa Teodor said. He showed her how tightening some spokes and loosening others would pull the rim back into a straight, even circle, so that the wheel spun without wobbling at all.

By the end of the month, the bicycle rolled smoothly down the driveway. Marisol offered to give it back, but her grandfather shook his head. "I rode it for forty years," he said. "It is time for me to relinquish it to someone who will ride it for forty more." He placed the key to the bike lock in her hand and let go.$q$, 'draft')
