-- Passage rg9-context-clues-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a0a4e800-e549-54e4-8287-9f85885ad87b', 'rg9-context-clues-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Kennel Nine$q$, $q$Kennel nine held a gray dog named Biscuit, and for her first week at the shelter, she would not come out. She was timid, flinching at every slammed door and hiding in the back corner whenever a volunteer walked by. Kwame, who volunteered at the shelter every Saturday, was told to be patient with her.

He started by sitting on the floor outside her kennel and reading a book aloud. Biscuit watched him from the corner. She was wary of him, keeping her eyes on his hands and backing away if he moved too quickly, as though she expected him to do something she would not like.

On the third Saturday, Kwame tried to coax her forward. He did not pull on a leash or raise his voice. Instead he placed small pieces of chicken in a line leading to the kennel door and waited, speaking softly, until she crept close enough to take the last piece from his palm.

Another volunteer, a tall teenager named Dmitri, saw Biscuit back away from a visiting family and told her sharply to stop being silly. The shelter manager pulled him aside afterward. She explained that a reprimand, even a mild scolding like that one, could undo weeks of progress with a frightened animal, because the dog would connect people with being punished.

By the end of the month, Biscuit trotted to the front of her kennel when Kwame arrived and leaned against his knees while he read. "She has turned a corner," the manager said, smiling. Two weeks later, a family with a quiet backyard took Biscuit home.$q$, 'draft')
